#{[17592186433044 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433044 #edb/ref 1069 "q-211244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433044 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433044 #edb/ref 1071 "$2\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\times 3=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433044 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b13fbae9-cf7a-599c-b37d-114ee2f7bfd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433044 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write $2\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{(2\\times 3)+2}{3}=\\frac{8}{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}\\times \\frac{3}{1}=\\frac{8\\times 3}{3\\times 1}=\\frac{24}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{3}=\\frac{24÷3}{3÷3}=\\frac{8}{1}=8\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433045 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433045 #edb/ref 1069 "q-211376" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433045 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433045 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed as a mixed number in its lowest terms, $2\\,\\frac{3}{4}\\times 6=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433045 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bae0062b-f5ac-5baa-b921-966c2cbaf487" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433045 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write $2\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+3}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $6$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n6=\\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{4}\\times \\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($4$) and the second fraction's numerator ($6$) have a common factor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{4}\\times \\frac{6}{1} & =\\frac{11}{1}\\times \\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =11\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\times 3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{33}{2}=16\\,\\text{R}\\,1=16\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433046 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433046 #edb/ref 1069 "q-211242" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433046 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433046 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed as a mixed number in its lowest terms, $3\\times 1\\,\\frac{5}{6}=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433046 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d47e615f-58e6-5902-88f8-098ef1f1c1bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433046 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{5}{6}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{5}{6}=\\frac{(1\\times 6)+5}{6}=\\frac{11}{6}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{11}{6}=\\frac{3\\times 11}{1\\times 6}=\\frac{33}{6}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{33}{6}=\\frac{33÷3}{6÷3}=\\frac{11}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{2}=5\\,\\text{R}\\,1=5\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433047 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433047 #edb/ref 1069 "q-57086" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433047 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433047 #edb/ref 1071 "$4\\times 2\\,\\frac{2}{5}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433047 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e01a2c34-8ac1-5c1d-8c31-0ae504dfb7c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433047 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4=\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\frac{2}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{(2\\times 5)+2}{5}=\\frac{12}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}\\times \\frac{12}{5}=\\frac{4\\times 12}{1\\times 5}=\\frac{48}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{48}{5}=9\\,\\text{R}3=9\\,\\frac{3}{5}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433048 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433048 #edb/ref 1069 "q-211208" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433048 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433048 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed as an improper fraction or mixed number in its lowest terms, $3\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\times 3=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433048 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f2ec4fda-aa3e-5894-ae68-873ea21a07ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433048 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write $3\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(3\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nWe also write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{1}=\\frac{7\\times 3}{2\\times 1}=\\frac{21}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can also write this improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{21}{2}=10\\,\\text{R}\\,1=10\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
